    selftest(board-drift): cover the HEAD-ancestry legs in §29
    
    `scripts/board_drift.py` runs its HEAD-ancestry leg only when `--published-dir`
    is absent (`if not args.no_head and not args.published_dir`). Every one of the
    seven existing §29 arms passed `--published-dir`, so the whole block — both the
    FAIL row and the PASS row — could be deleted and `bash tests/selftest.sh` still
    printed `SELFTEST: PASS`. The documented Git form (`--ref`, no published dir)
    had no arm at all, which contradicts AGENTS.md rule 2: a check that cannot fail
    is decoration.
    
    Added to §29, after the seven unchanged `--published-dir` arms, a scratch
    repository fixture (hermetic git: `-c user.email/user.name`, `commit.gpgsign=false`,
    `core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null`, `GIT_DIR`/`GIT_WORK_TREE`
    unset) whose board files are identical to the files at its own `main` ref. Two
    arms, both driving `board_drift.py check --workdir <repo> --ref main` with no
    `--published-dir`:
    
      PASS  git-form ancestry present (HEAD=<base> ancestor of main=<tip>) -> RESULT: OK
      PASS  git-form ancestry absent (HEAD=<sibling> not an ancestor of main=<tip>) -> RESULT: DRIFT
    
    The green arm detaches at the first commit and points `--ref` at `main` (the
    second commit). The red arm adds an empty sibling commit off the same base, so
    HEAD is not an ancestor of `--ref`; it asserts the full row
    `FAIL  HEAD ancestry — HEAD=<sibling> main=<tip>; ` (the measured pair, not just
    the label), `RESULT: DRIFT (1 failed)` exactly — every other row of the fixture
    is green, so no unrelated failure can make the arm pass — and exit 1. Both arms
    keep the section's convention that a `Traceback (most recent call last)` in the
    tool's output fails the arm.
    
    Criterion 3 control, measured this tick: a detached worktree of this commit
    carrying the new §29 with the tool's condition rewritten to `if False:` prints
    `SELFTEST: FAIL (2)` with `FAIL  git-form ancestry present (exit 0)` and
    `FAIL  git-form ancestry absent (exit 0)`. With the real tool the same tree
    prints `SELFTEST: PASS`. `scripts/board_drift.py` is unmodified here —
    sha256 b29b898f7b1d1c6358d396c561036dfc9ab6e78267bd96c9d47f79381a610477 in the
    worktree and in `git show HEAD:scripts/board_drift.py` alike.
    
    Observed on this host, same session, sequential runs:
    
      bash -n tests/selftest.sh              rc=0
      bash tests/selftest.sh                 SELFTEST: PASS, rc=0, 1m11.7s
      bash tests/selftest.sh (pre-change,    SELFTEST: PASS, rc=0, 1m12.6s
        pristine worktree at 51b8d32)
      bash scripts/boardctl validate         RESULT: OK (17 warning(s)), rc=0
      git status --porcelain -uall           M tests/selftest.sh
    
    Runtime delta: none measurable — 71.7s with the arms against 72.6s without them;
    they add two throwaway git repos and two tool invocations in an existing
    `mktemp -d`. The seven existing `--published-dir` arms are untouched and still
    print their seven PASS rows.
    
